	<logo>https://static.globalissues.org/i/globalissues/logo-feed.jpg</logo><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/11/01/44488</id><title>Breaking the silence in Mexico’s barracks</title><updated>2026-11-01T04:00:00-08:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/11/01/44488" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Libraries/Production%20Library/02-11-2022_Unsplash_journalist.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" /><media:content url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Libraries/Production%20Library/02-11-2022_Unsplash_journalist.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Libraries/Production%20Library/02-11-2022_Unsplash_journalist.jpg/image100x100cropped.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Libraries/Production%20Library/02-11-2022_Unsplash_journalist.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;In Mexico’s armed forces, an order from a superior is meant to be obeyed without question. For many women in uniform, investigative journalist Zorayda Gallegos found, that can include an order to step into an officer’s bedroom.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/11/01/44488&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “Breaking the silence in Mexico’s barracks”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/11/01/44487</id><title>COP17: Nations chart ‘Yerevan Pathway’ to speed up action for nature</title><updated>2026-11-01T04:00:00-08:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/11/01/44487" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Libraries/Production%20Library/31-05-2023_Agricultural%20Heritage%20Systems%203.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" /><media:content url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Libraries/Production%20Library/31-05-2023_Agricultural%20Heritage%20Systems%203.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Libraries/Production%20Library/31-05-2023_Agricultural%20Heritage%20Systems%203.jpg/image100x100cropped.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Libraries/Production%20Library/31-05-2023_Agricultural%20Heritage%20Systems%203.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;At the halfway mark to 2030, countries meeting in Armenia have agreed a roadmap to accelerate action for nature, after a global review found the world off track to meet its biodiversity targets.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/11/01/44487&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “COP17: Nations chart ‘Yerevan Pathway’ to speed up action for nature”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/11/01/44486</id><title>Western Sahara: UN ‘stands ready’ to keep supporting peace efforts as MINURSO mission ends</title><updated>2026-11-01T04:00:00-08:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/11/01/44486" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/assets/2018/12/06-12-2018-MINURSO-NICA440800.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" /><media:content url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/assets/2018/12/06-12-2018-MINURSO-NICA440800.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/assets/2018/12/06-12-2018-MINURSO-NICA440800.jpg/image100x100cropped.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/assets/2018/12/06-12-2018-MINURSO-NICA440800.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The UN remains committed to supporting a just, lasting and mutually acceptable political solution in Western Sahara that provides for the self-determination of its people, Secretary-General António Guterres said on Sunday, as the UN peacekeeping mission there began to wind down.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/11/01/44486&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “Western Sahara: UN ‘stands ready’ to keep supporting peace efforts as MINURSO mission ends”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/31/44485</id><title>‘They deserve to know the truth’: UN rights chief demands probe into staffer’s death in Houthi custody</title><updated>2026-10-31T05:00:00-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/31/44485" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/27-02-2026-HRC-Volker-Turk.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" /><media:content url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/27-02-2026-HRC-Volker-Turk.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/27-02-2026-HRC-Volker-Turk.jpg/image100x100cropped.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/27-02-2026-HRC-Volker-Turk.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The UN human rights chief on Saturday demanded an independent investigation into the death of a Yemeni colleague held by the Houthis for more than two years, whose family learned of his death only weeks after it happened.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/31/44485&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “‘They deserve to know the truth’: UN rights chief demands probe into staffer’s death in Houthi custody”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/31/44484</id><title>Somalia looks to the future as the UN's political mission closes</title><updated>2026-10-31T05:00:00-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/31/44484" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/2026/10/30-10-2026-UNTMIS-Somalia-03.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" /><media:content url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/2026/10/30-10-2026-UNTMIS-Somalia-03.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/2026/10/30-10-2026-UNTMIS-Somalia-03.jpg/image100x100cropped.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/2026/10/30-10-2026-UNTMIS-Somalia-03.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;“The story of the UN&#039;s political engagement in Somalia has been one of persistence, partnership and adaptation,” Raisedon Zenenga, the UN Secretary-General&#039;s Deputy Special Representative for Somalia has said, as three decades of UN political missions in the Horn of Africa country comes to an end on 31 October.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/31/44484&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “Somalia looks to the future as the UN&#039;s political mission closes”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/31/44483</id><title>Samarkand: A ‘living museum’ on the Silk Road grapples with a tourism boom</title><updated>2026-10-31T05:00:00-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/31/44483" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/10/29-10-2026_Samarkand.jpg/image1440x560cropped.jpg" /><media:content url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/10/29-10-2026_Samarkand.jpg/image1440x560cropped.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/10/29-10-2026_Samarkand.jpg/image100x100cropped.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/10/29-10-2026_Samarkand.jpg/image1440x560cropped.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;As the sun sets over Samarkand’s majestic Registan, its last rays glint across the turquoise mosaics. Dusk settles on the ancient square where great trade routes have crossed for centuries.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/31/44483&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “Samarkand: A ‘living museum’ on the Silk Road grapples with a tourism boom”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/31/44482</id><title>DPR Korea: Isolation deepens even as the country changes, says UN rights official</title><updated>2026-10-31T05:00:00-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/31/44482" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/10/26-10-2026_james_heenan.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" /><media:content url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/10/26-10-2026_james_heenan.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/10/26-10-2026_james_heenan.jpg/image100x100cropped.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/10/26-10-2026_james_heenan.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Democratic People’s Republic of Korea (DPRK) is building factories and hospitals, rolling out smartphones and mobile banking, and passing new laws at an unprecedented rate. But its people are more cut off from the outside world than at any point in the country’s history.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/31/44482&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “DPR Korea: Isolation deepens even as the country changes, says UN rights official”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/30/44478</id><title>Green Cities Can Make Room for New Homes and the Farms That Feed Them</title><updated>2026-10-30T18:22:36-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/30/44478" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/10/urbanagriculture.jpg" /><media:content url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/10/urbanagriculture.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/10/urbanagriculture-100x100.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/10/urbanagriculture.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;
ROME, October 30 (IPS)  - A field at the edge of a growing city may be worth far more than its market price suggests. It may produce vegetables sold in nearby markets, support local livelihoods and provide green space in an increasingly built-up environment. It can also help cities manage water, adapt to climate shocks and strengthen local food supplies. As municipal authorities decide where new homes and infrastructure should go, understanding what their land already contributes can help them plan for housing and food together.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/30/44478&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “Green Cities Can Make Room for New Homes and the Farms That Feed Them”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/30/44477</id><title>African Athletes Should be Free to be Themselves</title><updated>2026-10-30T15:34:34-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/30/44477" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/10/castersemenya.jpg" /><media:content url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/10/castersemenya.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/10/castersemenya-100x100.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/10/castersemenya.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;
DAKAR, October 30 (IPS)  - Senegal is welcoming athletes and visitors from across the globe as part of the &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.olympics.com/ioc/dakar-2026&quot;&gt;4th Summer Youth Olympic Games&lt;/a&gt;, the first-ever Olympics in Africa. Lesbian, gay, bisexual and transgender (LGBT) African athletes, including from &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.seneweb.com/fr/news/Sport/halba-diouf-lathlete-transgenre-franco-senegalaise-qui-defie-les-regles-ettrump_n_472878.html&quot;&gt;Senegal&lt;/a&gt;, have repeatedly &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.them.us/story/cindy-ngamba-olympics-team-refugee-bronze-medal-lesbian-boxer&quot;&gt;participated in and won&lt;/a&gt; competitions–while advocating their rights to compete and speaking out &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.hrw.org/news/2025/07/15/global-caster-semenyas-court-victory-a-win-for-all-athletes&quot;&gt;against discrimination&lt;/a&gt;.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/30/44477&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “African Athletes Should be Free to be Themselves”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/30/44474</id><title>New Biodiversity Pledges Emerge at COP17, But Funding Gap Persists</title><updated>2026-10-30T13:08:00-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/30/44474" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/10/GEF-DG.jpeg" /><media:content url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/10/GEF-DG.jpeg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/10/GEF-DG-100x100.jpeg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/10/GEF-DG.jpeg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;
YEREVAN, Armenia, October 30 (IPS)  - The second week of COP17 was dominated by discussions on biodiversity finance, with new pledges announced and donor outreach intensifying. Yet as the summit approached its close, the financing gap for implementing the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework (KMGBF) remains largely unchanged.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/10/30/44474&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “New Biodiversity Pledges Emerge at COP17, But Funding Gap Persists”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry></feed><!-- 0.0014s ch:1 -->